  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Time-Shifting Property of the Laplace Transform",
    "lesson_core_move": "Apply the one-sided Laplace time-shifting property to convert delayed signals written as $f(t-a)u(t-a)$ into $e^{-as}F(s)$ so unit-step and piecewise-signal transforms can be handled algebraically.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Shifting Property of the Laplace Transform",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time-Shifting Property of the Laplace Transform",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The one-sided Laplace transform works with causal signals, so it is written around $u(t)$ turning a signal on at and after zero. This means the signal form should explicitly include the one-sided gate, which is why we first identify the delay using $f(t-a)u(t-a)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The rule is: if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)u(t)\\}=F(s)$, then $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t-a)u(t-a)\\}=e^{-as}F(s)$ for $a\\ge 0$. Here $a$ is the delay to the right and $F(s)$ is the transform of the undelayed base signal $f(t)u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t-a)u(t-a)\\}=e^{-as}F(s),\\quad a\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base case $f(t)=1$ with $\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t)\\}=1/s$. With delay $a$, the delayed constant is $u(t-a)$, so the transform becomes $\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a)\\}=e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t)\\}=e^{-as}/s$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a)\\}=\\frac{e^{-as}}{s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Before applying the rule, check two things: first, the delayed signal is in exact form $f(t-a)u(t-a)$ with the same shift $a$ in both the base term and the step, and second, the delay satisfies $a\\ge 0$ for the one-sided formula.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Shifting Property of the Laplace Transform",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Base Signal and Delay",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-3)(t-3)^2\\}$ by rewriting it in the delayed form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Treat this as a delay-identification move. The target form is $x(t)=f(t-a)u(t-a)$. Here $x(t)=u(t-3)(t-3)^2$, so the same delay $a=3$ appears in both the base argument and the step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "First rewrite the delayed base term as $f(t-3)$: set $f(t)=t^2$. Then we can express the signal as $x(t)=f(t-3)u(t-3)$, which is the exact template required by the theorem.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=u(t-3)(t-3)^2=f(t-3)u(t-3),\\quad f(t)=t^2.",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the known one-sided pair for the base signal: $\\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\}=\\frac{2}{s^3}$. Because this is the delayed version with $a=3$, the delay factor is $e^{-3s}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t-3)u(t-3)\\}=e^{-3s}F(s),\\quad F(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)u(t)\\}=\\frac{2}{s^3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute and report the result as $\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-3)(t-3)^2\\}=\\dfrac{2e^{-3s}}{s^3}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Shifting Property of the Laplace Transform",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Applying the Property from a Known Base Transform",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Use the known pair $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-2t}u(t)\\}=1/(s+2)$ to compute $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-2(t-1)}u(t-1)\\}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is a cue-based reuse step: the base transform is already known, so we do not rederive $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-2t}u(t)\\}$. We only need to identify the delay structure and apply the time-shifting factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match the target to the one-sided delayed template $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t-a)u(t-a)\\}=e^{-as}F(s)$. The signal can be written as $f(t-1)u(t-1)$ with $f(t)=e^{-2t}$ and delay $a=1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-2(t-1)}u(t-1)=f(t-1)u(t-1),\\quad f(t)=e^{-2t},\\quad a=1.",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From the given cue, we have the base transform $F(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)u(t)\\}=\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-2t}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+2}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the delay multiplier once and only once: multiply by $e^{-as}$ with $a=1$ to account for the shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-2(t-1)}u(t-1)\\}=e^{-s} \\cdot F(s)=\\frac{e^{-s}}{s+2}.",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Result: $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-2(t-1)}u(t-1)\\}=\\frac{e^{-s}}{s+2}$. This stays in one-sided causal form because the delay is cleanly separated from the same base transform.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Shifting Property of the Laplace Transform",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Computing the Base Transform Before Adding the Delay Factor",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $L\\{u(t-4)(t-4+2)\\}$ by first finding the base transform of $t+2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Treat this as the sequencing move: compute the non-delayed base transform first, then attach the shift factor at the end. The target signal is $x(t)=u(t-4)(t-4+2)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match $x(t)$ to the delayed template $f(t-a)u(t-a)$. Rewrite the bracketed polynomial as a delayed base function: $x(t)=f(t-4)u(t-4)$ with $f(t)=t+2$ and $a=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=u(t-4)(t-4+2)=\\bigl(t-4+2\\bigr)u(t-4)=f(t-4)u(t-4),\\quad f(t)=t+2,\\quad a=4.",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now find the base transform of the undelayed base signal: $F(s)=L\\{f(t)u(t)\\}=L\\{t+2\\}=\\frac{1}{s^2}+\\frac{2}{s}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the one-sided shift only after the base is ready: the transform rule gives a multiplicative factor $e^{-as}$ with $a=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "L\\{u(t-4)f(t-4)\\}=e^{-4s}F(s),\\quad F(s)=\\frac{1}{s^2}+\\frac{2}{s},\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad L\\{u(t-4)(t-4+2)\\}=e^{-4s}\\left(\\frac{1}{s^2}+\\frac{2}{s}\\right).",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: $L\\{u(t-4)(t-4+2)\\}=\\frac{e^{-4s}}{s^2}+\\frac{2e^{-4s}}{s}$. This keeps the delay $e^{-4s}$ as a separate final multiplier, which is the required sequencing pattern.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Shifting Property of the Laplace Transform",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing When the Delayed Form Is Ready for Direct Use",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Determine whether $x(t)=u(t-1)(t+1)^2$ is directly ready for the theorem, then compute $L\\{x(t)\\}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Before applying the shift rule, run the readiness check: can the signal be written as $f(t-a)u(t-a)$? If not, perform one local-time rewrite and then apply the theorem.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $x(t)=u(t-1)(t+1)^2$. Use the local-time variable $\\tau=t-1$. Then $x(t)=u(t-1)(\\tau+2)^2$ and this is already in delayed form as $f(\\tau)u(t-1)$ with $a=1$ and $f(\\tau)=(\\tau+2)^2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=u(t-1)(t+1)^2=u(t-1)(t-1+2)^2=u(t-1)f(t-1),\\quad f(t)= (t+2)^2.",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To keep one-sided notation stable, the base transform is $F(s)=L\\{f(t)u(t)\\}=L\\{(t+2)^2\\}=L\\{t^2+4t+4\\}=\\frac{2}{s^3}+\\frac{4}{s^2}+\\frac{4}{s}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now attach the delay factor from the one-sided time-shifting rule exactly once: multiply by $e^{-as}$ with $a=1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "L\\{x(t)\\}=L\\{u(t-1)(t+1)^2\\}=e^{-s}F(s)=e^{-s}\\left(\\frac{2}{s^3}+\\frac{4}{s^2}+\\frac{4}{s}\\right).",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: $L\\{u(t-1)(t+1)^2\\}=e^{-s}\\left(\\frac{2}{s^3}+\\frac{4}{s^2}+\\frac{4}{s}\\right)=\\frac{2e^{-s}}{s^3}+\\frac{4e^{-s}}{s^2}+\\frac{4e^{-s}}{s}$. The key decision is that the signal became valid after one local-time rewrite check.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
